When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is added to a solution containing \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the presence of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) forms \( \text{HS}^- \) and \( \text{S}^{2-} \) ions, which react with the metal ions to form metal sulfides.  
The solubility of metal sulfides decreases with increasing charge and decreasing size.  
\[
\text{M}^{n+} + x \text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{MS}_x + 2x\text{H}^+
\]
The solubility product \( K_{sp} \) for the metal sulfides is:
\[
K_{sp} = [\text{M}^{n+}]^n [\text{S}^{2-}]^{\frac{x}{2}}
\]
\[
\boxed{\text{Mn}^{2+}} \quad \text{MnS} \quad K_{sp} = 2.3 \times 10^{-16}
\]
\[
\boxed{\text{Ni}^{2+}} \quad \text{NiS} \downarrow \quad K_{sp} = 3 \times 10^{-26}
\]
